{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Pseudo-Circular Data References in bpy.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Pseudo-Circular Data References in bpy.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"pseudo circular data references in bpy pseudo circular data references in bpy","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Pseudo-Circular Data References in bpy.md","fileName":"Pseudo-Circular Data References in bpy.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Pseudo-Circular Data References in bpy","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-08 09:04\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Blender Python Data and Operations]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Blender Python Data and Operations","blenderpythonapi.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Pseudo-Circular Data References in bpy.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Pseudo-Circular Data References in bpy.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"pseudo circular data references in bpy pseudo circular data references in bpy","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Pseudo-Circular Data References in bpy.md","fileName":"Pseudo-Circular Data References in bpy.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Pseudo-Circular Data References in bpy","heading":"Pseudo-Circular Data References in bpy","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Blender's Python data model allows related structures to refer through one another. An object can expose its mesh through `.data`, while nested properties can lead back toward owning or contextual structures. These paths can look circular even though they are controlled API references rather than unrestricted Python object cycles.\n\nThe benefit is abstraction: code can begin with the entity it has and navigate to the data it needs. The limitation is that not every apparent reverse path exists or permits assignment. Developers should inspect actual members